218:
Among those suttas, what is the establishment of the faculty of wisdom (paññindriyādhiṭṭhāna)?

The sutta stating, "Wisdom is indeed the best in the world," should be understood in detail. Monks, there are these three kinds of wisdom: wisdom based on learning (sutamaya-paññā), wisdom based on reflection (cintāmaya-paññā), and wisdom based on mental development (bhāvanāmaya-paññā). This is the sutta on the establishment of the faculty of wisdom. These suttas on the establishment of the faculties are unshared (asādhāraṇa) suttas.

Among those suttas, what are the shared (sādhāraṇa) suttas on the establishment of the faculties?

For one whose lust for sensual pleasures is not yet gone, their five faculties are weak. Faith, mindfulness, energy, tranquility (samatha), and insight (vipassanā) are present. By clinging to such a monk (with weak faculties), one is destroyed beforehand.

The five faculties are to be understood as the faculty of faith and so on. The sutta should be expanded upon the three types of unshakable confidence (aveccappasāda) in the Three Jewels. These are the shared suttas on the establishment of the faculties. Whichever sutta is connected to the wholesome or the unwholesome should be pointed out by that respective establishment; there is no other Dhamma to be pointed out. In that teaching, there are shared wholesome states and unwholesome states that are not called wholesome. Whichever shared wholesome roots or shared unwholesome roots arise, they abandon sensual thoughts (kāmavitakka)... [and so on]... the four right efforts are both wholesome and unwholesome.

## The Summary Verses (Uddānagāthā) for those suttas are:

The sutta on the person who greatly desires through sensual thoughts and the person who is dear because of giving;
The sutta on the one who takes life, and the sutta teaching the three characteristics of a fool of such a person.
The enjoyment of benefits for a period of a hundred or a thousand years; those ascetics and brahmins;
The sutta teaching the going to wrong courses through the four biases: favoritism, hatred, fear, and delusion.
One who dwells frequently contemplating the foul (asubha), and the attention to the foul in objects;
If one should love even a single being, and the sutta on if friends should speak well-spoken words.
Wisdom is indeed the best in the world; the three faculties beginning with the faculty of 'I shall know the unknown' (anaññātaññassāmītindriya);
The sutta teaching the wholesome and unwholesome roots, and the arising of the comparable and incomparable (tula-atula).
One should perform wholesome deeds with the body, along with the three types of good conduct;
The virtuous say well-spoken words are best; and the suttas teaching the verbal good conducts.
